      {
        "id": "functional-annotation",
        "says": "@FunctionalInterface is optional and only asks the compiler to enforce the one-abstract-method rule; it has RUNTIME retention.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Best Practices and Optimization Techniques"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "the compiler's check cannot be graded at run time and reading the annotation is a single call; functional-check grades the rule the annotation enforces"
      },
      {
        "id": "functional-sam",
        "says": "A functional interface has exactly one abstract method: default, static and Object's public methods do not count, and inherited abstract methods do.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Edge Cases and Their Handling",
          "example:42-annotations/README_10.4.1.md:12",
          "example:42-annotations/README_10.4.1.md:13"
        ],
        "exercise": "functional-check",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "heap-pollution",
        "says": "Storing a wrongly typed value into a generic varargs array pollutes the heap, and the ClassCastException appears later at a read.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "the page shows it as code not to write; safe-varargs and checked-cast grade the safe side of the same idea"
      },
      {
        "id": "java21-usage",
